    level: "C1",
    text: "Colours are one of the first vocabulary sets every language learner masters, yet English has a remarkably rich and nuanced colour vocabulary. The primary colours — red, blue, and yellow — combine to create secondary colours: orange (red + yellow), green (blue + yellow), and purple (red + blue). Black and white are considered achromatic — without colour — while grey sits between them. Brown, pink, and turquoise are common colours that don't fit neatly into the primary-secondary system. English distinguishes between light and dark shades: light blue versus dark blue, pale green versus deep green. Colours can be described as bright, vivid, bold, and vibrant for intensity, or muted, dull, pastel, and faded for softness. The colour spectrum follows the rainbow: red, orange, yellow, green, blue, indigo, and violet — remembered by the mnemonic 'Roy G. Biv'. Warm colours (reds, oranges, yellows) evoke energy and heat, while cool colours (blues, greens, purples) suggest calm and nature. Neutral colours — beige, cream, ivory, taupe — are popular in fashion and interior design. Metallic colours like gold, silver, and bronze add luxury. Cultural associations vary enormously: white symbolises purity in Western culture but mourning in some Eastern cultures; green represents nature but also envy. Mastering colour vocabulary enhances descriptions of art, fashion, nature, and everyday objects.",
